#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""sharedbox-canary: decoy "secrets" tripwire. Whoever opens them is recorded, emailed about, and frozen.

  sharedbox-canary plant          create the decoys in CANARY_DIR (idempotent; never overwrites)   [root]
  sharedbox-canary watch          the daemon (sharedbox-canary.service)                            [root]
  sharedbox-canary thaw USER      unfreeze a user frozen by the tripwire                           [root]
  sharedbox-canary status         decoys + recent hits                                             [root]
  sharedbox-canary test           how to check that the tripwire works

CANARY_DIR (default /srv/backups/prod) holds world-readable fake secrets: a .env with live-looking
Stripe/OpenAI keys, AWS credentials, an SSH deploy key (real ed25519, authorized nowhere), a users
SQL dump and a README. Nothing legitimate ever reads them, so an open means someone is looking for
secrets on this box. The daemon watches them with fanotify (open permission events: the opener is
held for a few milliseconds while we read who it is from /proc, then always allowed, so they take
the bait), and then:

  CANARY_ACTION=freeze (default)  friend accounts (uid FRIEND_UID_MIN..MAX, not admins) are frozen:
                                  `systemctl freeze user-UID.slice` pauses every process of theirs in
                                  place for forensics. Reversible: sudo sharedbox-canary thaw USER
  CANARY_ACTION=alert             record + email only
  root/admin openers are only ever alerted on. One freeze+email per user per 10 minutes; further
  hits are counted.

Hits go to /var/lib/sharedbox/canary.jsonl (read by the security audit) and events.jsonl.

Better AWS bait: get a free AWS key canary at https://canarytokens.org (it emails YOU whenever the
key is used anywhere on the internet, so you learn about it even if the thief copies it off-box)
and save its credentials file as /etc/sharedbox/canary/aws-credentials. `plant` uses it instead of
the fake one (replacing the fake one it planted earlier, never a file you put there yourself).
README.txt and the .pub key are bait only (reading a README is curiosity, not theft); the other
files are the tripwires.
"""

